## The temple at the end of the desert road.

Abu Simbel is not on the way to anywhere. It sits two hundred and eighty kilometres south of Aswan, close to the Sudanese border, on the shore of Lake Nasser, and that distance is part of its power. You drive out through an empty desert as the sky lightens, and then, round the side of a hill, there they are: four seated kings, each the height of a six-storey building, carved straight from the mountain.

Ramesses II had them cut here to face the sunrise and to be seen by everyone who came north up the Nile from Nubia. More than three thousand years later, they still do the job.

## Inside the mountain

Step between the colossi and the mountain opens into a great hall lined with statues of Ramesses as Osiris. On the walls, his battle against the Hittites at Kadesh unfolds in carved and painted scenes: the camp, the chariots, the king in the thick of it. Deeper in, through smaller halls, is the sanctuary, where four seated gods wait in the dark. Twice a year, around 22 February and 22 October, the rising sun reaches all the way in to light them.

Next door stands the temple Ramesses made for Hathor and for his great royal wife, Nefertari. Along its facade her statues stand as tall as his, a rare honour for a queen, and inside, Hathor and Nefertari appear again and again in softly coloured relief.

> He built it to be seen from the river by every traveller coming north. It still stops you where you stand.

## A mountain moved

When the High Dam was built, Lake Nasser would have drowned both temples. Between 1964 and 1968, an international effort cut them into great blocks, lifted them more than sixty metres and rebuilt them inside an artificial mountain, aligned so the sun would still find the sanctuary. Look closely and you may spot the joins.

## Left to us

The day starts early and we make that part easy: the car at your Aswan hotel, coffee and breakfast for the road, and time to sleep as the desert goes by. The road, the tickets, the timing: your concierge takes care of it, with local knowledge and our craft. You are back in Aswan for a late lunch on the river, with the rest of the afternoon to yourselves. If you'd rather fly, or stay the night, we arrange that too.
